<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating theoretical probability for a token drawing experiment</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A mathematics teacher in Kisumu prepares a cloth bag containing coloured plastic tokens for a probability experiment. The bag contains <b>{@num_red@} red tokens</b> and <b>{@num_blue@} blue tokens</b>.</p>

[[jsxgraph width="400px" height="300px"]]
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-2, 11, 12, -1],
    axis: false, grid: false, showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false, pan: {enabled: false}, zoom: {enabled: false}
});

/* Draw the outline of the cloth bag */
board.create('polygon', [[0,0], [10,0], [11.5,10], [-1.5,10]], {
    fillColor: '#d7ccc8', fillOpacity: 0.6, 
    borders: {strokeWidth: 3, strokeColor: '#8d6e63'}
});
board.create('text', [5, -0.5, 'Bag of Tokens'], {anchorX: 'middle', fontSize: 14, strokeColor: '#5d4037'});

/* Dynamically scatter the exact number of red and blue tokens */
var red_c = {#num_red#};
var blue_c = {#num_blue#};

for(var i=0; i<red_c; i++) {
    /* Use modulo math to create a pseudo-random but stable scatter pattern */
    var rx = 1.5 + (i * 3.7) % 7;
    var ry = 1.5 + (i * 2.3) % 7;
    board.create('point', [rx, ry], {name:'', size: 8, fillColor: '#f44336', strokeColor: '#b71c1c'});
}

for(var j=0; j<blue_c; j++) {
    var bx = 1.5 + (j * 4.1 + 2) % 7;
    var by = 1.5 + (j * 1.9 + 3.5) % 7;
    board.create('point', [bx, by], {name:'', size: 8, fillColor: '#2196f3', strokeColor: '#0d47a1'});
}
[[/jsxgraph]]

<p>A student is asked to close their eyes and draw exactly <b>{@draw_total@} tokens</b> simultaneously from the bag.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Calculate the total number of possible combinations for drawing {@draw_total@} tokens from the entire bag.</p>
<p>\(n(S) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Calculate the number of possible outcomes where the student draws <i>exactly</i> <b>{@req_red@} red token(s)</b> and <b>{@req_blue@} blue token(s)</b>.</p>
<p>\(n(E) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Using your results from parts (a) and (b), determine the theoretical probability of this exact combination occurring.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{exact combination}) =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: The Sample Space</b><br>
The bag contains a total of \({@num_red@} + {@num_blue@} = {@total_tokens@}\) tokens. We are drawing a subset of \({@draw_total@}\) tokens. Using the combinations formula:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle n(S) = {}^{{@total_tokens@}}C_{{@draw_total@}} = \frac{{@total_tokens@}!}{{@draw_total@}!({@total_tokens@} - {@draw_total@})!} = {@ta1@} \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: The Favourable Outcomes</b><br>
The event requires choosing exactly \({@req_red@}\) token(s) from the \({@num_red@}\) red tokens, AND \({@req_blue@}\) token(s) from the \({@num_blue@}\) blue tokens. Because these are drawn from two distinct groups, we multiply their independent combinations:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle n(E) = {}^{{@num_red@}}C_{{@req_red@}} \times {}^{{@num_blue@}}C_{{@req_blue@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle n(E) = {@red_comb@} \times {@blue_comb@} = {@ta2@} \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: The Theoretical Probability</b><br>
Theoretical probability is defined as the number of favourable outcomes divided by the total number of equally-likely outcomes in the sample space:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(E) = \frac{n(E)}{n(S)} = \frac{{@ta2@}}{{@ta1@}} \)</p>
<p>Simplifying this fraction mathematically gives the final probability:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ {@ta3@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
